      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.048,
            0.419,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.845,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "to be detailed enough so that one can physically construct the objects they depict."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.126,
            0.772,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "We will now explore some ways of drawing solids on a plane."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.156,
            0.278,
            0.175
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Projections"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.18,
            0.846,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": "The net of a solid (when it exists) is one way of representing a solid on the plane. But it can be difficult to visualise the solid from the net, without physically cutting out the net and folding it up."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.236,
            0.847,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "Another way to represent a solid is by looking at the **projections** of all its points on a plane. This idea is closely related to the profile of a solid from a specific viewpoint, which we discussed earlier. Let us see how this technique works."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.307,
            0.666,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.548,
            0.845,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "Consider a point P in space, and a plane M. Imagine a line from P intersecting the plane M at a point O."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.585,
            0.847,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "We say that OP is **perpendicular to the plane** if for any line OX on the plane, $\\angle POX = 90^{\\circ}$ In this case, we say that point O is the **projection** of point P on the plane. The projections of all the points of an object together form the **projection of the object on the plane**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.669,
            0.534,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "? Let us visualise the projection of a line."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.691,
            0.813,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": "",
